the european central bank has been  conducting an experiment for nearly a  decade now they had negative interest  rates and that monetary policy the whole  idea was to constantly ease the  environment they wanted more and more  people to be able to borrow cheap  capital on top of that they wanted to  actually incentivize investment and all  sorts of monetary velocity in the  european economy now of course as we  were in good times that was a great  policy and they kept the party going but  now that all of a sudden asset prices  are crashing recession is looming on the  horizon pressure is coming on the  european central bank will they keep  their interest rate negative or will  they actually capitulate and change  strategy after almost a decade will the  federal reserve in the united states  they've gone ahead and started to  increase the interest rate they've done  so fairly aggressively but the ecb  christine lagarde the president they've  been holding out over the last couple of  months they said that we're gonna break  from the federal reserve we are not  going to actually do what they're gonna  do we're gonna stick to our plan well  ladies and gentlemen as these things  usually go christine lagarde and the ecb  they're changing their tune just this  morning they've gone ahead and they said  that they're actually gonna go and  they're gonna replicate what the federal  reserve's doing they're gonna increase  their interest rate they're gonna bring  it back to zero it's comical that that  aggressive move from negative interest  rates to zero so controversial but they  went further on and they said that  they're actually going to continue to  increase interest rates and they'll  probably bring them back positive again  in europe over the coming months this  means that the ecb and the federal  reserve in lockstep are going to  continue to increase interest rates as  interest rates goes up capital becomes  more expensive and asset prices  especially those in growth s